# `material`/`matref` (docs/bzw.md, "Materials and appearance"): a named
# bundle of a stock texture and a tint, pulled in by name instead of
# restating `addtexture`/`diffuse` on every obstacle that wants it. This one
# swaps the plain `boxwall` for upstream's own `caution` texture and tints it
# the same amber the bad-flag ring above is painted, so both read as the same
# warning colour -- one from a `color` line, this one from a material.
material
  name caution-amber
  addtexture caution
  diffuse 1.0 0.62745 0.12549
end

box
  name matref
  position 135 -125 0
  rotation 0
  size 6.0 6.0 5.0
  matref caution-amber
end

# `pos`/`rot`, `WorldFileLocation::read`'s aliases of `position`/`rotation`,
# and `flipz`, the other way of saying a negative size height.
box
  name aliased
  pos 65 -108 0
  rot 45.0
  size 6.0 6.0 5.0
end

pyramid
  name flipped
  position 79 -108 0
  FlipZ
  rotation 0
  size 6.0 6.0 5.0
end

# South-west: the same teleporter pair `flagbuffet.bzw` carries, and two
# overhangs to jump into. `doUpdateMotion` zeroes a tank's rise the instant
# it meets a surface above it (see AGENTS.md), so driving under either of
# these and jumping is what that rule looks like from the driver's seat --
# the tank falls back to the ground rather than sticking to the underside.
box
  name platform
  position -95 -95 0
  size 10 10 8
end

teleporter portal
  position -120 -95 0
  rotation 90
end

teleporter roof
  position -95 -95 8
  rotation 90
end

link
  from portal:f
  to   roof:b
end

link
  from roof:b
  to   portal:f
end

link
  from portal:b
  to   roof:f
end

link
  from roof:f
  to   portal:b
end

# A flat ceiling to jump into: four units of clearance, easily driven under,
# and well inside a jump's apex.
box
  name jump_box
  position -115 -70 4
  size 6 6 3
end

# The same test against a pyramid's base rather than a box's underside --
# raised clear of the ground, so the base is a real face rather than the one
# `public/face-trim.mjs` would otherwise leave out.
pyramid
  name jump_pyramid
  position -95 -70 4
  size 6 6 4
end

# A doorway frame built from three plain boxes -- two pillars and a header --
# sized to a default teleporter's outer footprint (w 1.12, d 13.44, h 21.28,
# border 1.12; see BZW_TELEPORTER_DEFAULTS and CustomGate::finalize in
# server.js). Plain boxes with the same thin, long footprint exercise
# resolveTankMotion's generic corner-slide handling directly, independent of
# the teleporter's own jamb/header code, and can be dropped into bzfs/bzflag
# unmodified for comparison against upstream.
box
  name frame_pillar_a
  position -135 -63.84 0
  size 0.56 0.56 20.16
end

box
  name frame_pillar_b
  position -135 -76.16 0
  size 0.56 0.56 20.16
end

box
  name frame_header
  position -135 -70 20.16
  size 0.56 6.72 1.12
end

# `define`/`group`, clear of the corner tests and the base ring. One
# definition -- a two-box watchtower -- placed twice, so the composed
# transform (`docs/bzw.md`, "Groups") is visible rather than asserted: the
# second instance is turned 45 degrees and stretched taller, and neither copy
# names its own boxes, so the per-instance naming that keeps `Base`/`Cap`
# from colliding between the two (`Watchtower#0:Base`, `Watchtower#1:Base`,
# ...) is on display in the debug label too.
define Watchtower
box
  name Base
  position 0 0 0
  size 3 3 6
end
box
  name Cap
  position 0 0 6
  size 4 4 1
end
enddef

group Watchtower
  position -120 -130 0
end

group Watchtower
  position -108 -118 0
  rotation 45
  size 1 1 1.5
end

# Mesh geometry, clear of the corner tests and the watchtowers. One of each
# of the three shapes upstream's own wiki.bzflag.org/Mesh examples carry --
# a solid cube, a texcoord'd two-sided billboard, and a jump-through floor --
# so the render and collision paths (`docs/bzw-plan.md`'s "Mesh geometry")
# have real content to check themselves against in the client, not only in
# `parseBZWMap`. A tank stops at the cube and the floor's own solid top face,
# slides along either the way it would off a box corner, and the floor's own
# underside and the billboard bush below are both passable.

mesh
  name mesh_cube
  vertex -73 -73 0
  vertex -67 -73 0
  vertex -67 -67 0
  vertex -73 -67 0
  vertex -73 -73 6
  vertex -67 -73 6
  vertex -67 -67 6
  vertex -73 -67 6
  texture boxwall
  face
    vertices 0 1 5 4
  endface
  face
    vertices 1 2 6 5
  endface
  face
    vertices 2 3 7 6
  endface
  face
    vertices 3 0 4 7
  endface
  face
    vertices 0 1 2 3
  endface
  face
    vertices 4 5 6 7
    texture roof
  endface
end

# A real map's own alpha-blended texture (a shrub cutout, genuinely
# transparent where there is no leaf, not just an unused alpha channel --
# confirmed directly against the live image), rather than a stock asset bzo
# already knows is opaque -- so this billboard also exercises the
# alpha-detection path in `public/texture.js`/`public/render.js`, not only
# mesh collision and rendering. `passable` on both faces, the same as a real
# bush or a small tree upstream's own mapper community builds this way --
# decorative, never a wall.
define billboard_bush_quad
  mesh
    vertex -3 0 0
    vertex 3 0 0
    vertex 3 0 6
    vertex -3 0 6
    texcoord 0 0
    texcoord 1 0
    texcoord 1 1
    texcoord 0 1
    texture http://images.bzflag.org/bz_legacy/trepan/tiles/shrub.png
    # The settings every other map using this exact picture states for it --
    # six of the seven found in a survey of live public servers agree on all
    # of it, so this is the mapper community's own recipe for a billboard
    # cutout rather than a guess. `alphathresh 0.7` is the one that matters:
    # with no threshold stated bzo falls back to its own 0.05 and upstream
    # runs no alpha test at all, so the same quad renders two ways.
    # `noshadow` is the one bzo still only reads and reports on load.
    alphathresh 0.7
    noculling
    nosorting
    noradar
    noshadow
    nolighting
    passable
    face
      vertices 0 1 2 3
      texcoords 0 1 2 3
    endface
    face #backside
      vertices 1 0 3 2
      texcoords 1 0 3 2
    endface
  end
enddef

# The same quad with its texture mirrored left-right (`texcoord`'s own U
# flipped at each corner, geometry and winding otherwise identical) -- eight
# copies of one leaf pattern all facing the same way reads as an obvious
# repeat from any angle that lands between two planes; alternating this one
# in breaks that symmetry up the same way real foliage sprites do.
define billboard_bush_quad_mirrored
  mesh
    vertex -3 0 0
    vertex 3 0 0
    vertex 3 0 6
    vertex -3 0 6
    texcoord 1 0
    texcoord 0 0
    texcoord 0 1
    texcoord 1 1
    texture http://images.bzflag.org/bz_legacy/trepan/tiles/shrub.png
    # Same material settings as the quad above.
    alphathresh 0.7
    noculling
    nosorting
    noradar
    noshadow
    nolighting
    passable
    face
      vertices 0 1 2 3
      texcoords 0 1 2 3
    endface
    face #backside
      vertices 1 0 3 2
      texcoords 1 0 3 2
    endface
  end
enddef

# Eight instances of that one quad, sharing one vertical axis and each turned
# 22.5 degrees further than the last -- placed here, all at this definition's
# own local origin, so the whole cluster is one reusable shape a single
# outer `group` (below) can then stand wherever the map wants a bush, the
# same nested-definition recursion `import-Planet-MoFo.com_4202.bzw`'s own
# `base_pillar` (a `define` wrapping `group base_oval` instances) already
# proved out earlier this session -- rather than repeating the same shift on
# every one of the eight. A two-sided quad looks identical to its own
# 180-degree turn, so eight steps of 22.5 degrees covers every distinct
# orientation there is -- going all the way to 360 would just redraw the
# same four planes a second time. A handful of crossed flat planes reads as
# a rounded, roughly volumetric bush from any angle despite each one being
# perfectly flat -- the same fake-foliage technique real maps use, and
# cheaper than a real 3D bush mesh would be.
define billboard_bush
  group billboard_bush_quad
  end
  group billboard_bush_quad_mirrored
    rotation 22.5
  end
  group billboard_bush_quad
    rotation 45
  end
  group billboard_bush_quad_mirrored
    rotation 67.5
  end
  group billboard_bush_quad
    rotation 90
  end
  group billboard_bush_quad_mirrored
    rotation 112.5
  end
  group billboard_bush_quad
    rotation 135
  end
  group billboard_bush_quad_mirrored
    rotation 157.5
  end
enddef

group billboard_bush
  shift -57 -70 0
end

mesh
  name mesh_jump_through_floor
  vertex -73 -63 3
  vertex -67 -63 3
  vertex -67 -57 3
  vertex -73 -57 3
  vertex -73 -63 6
  vertex -67 -63 6
  vertex -67 -57 6
  vertex -73 -57 6
  texture pyrwall
  face
    vertices 0 1 5 4
    drivethrough
  endface
  face
    vertices 1 2 6 5
    drivethrough
  endface
  face
    vertices 2 3 7 6
    drivethrough
  endface
  face
    vertices 3 0 4 7
    drivethrough
  endface
  face
    vertices 0 1 2 3
    drivethrough
  endface
  face
    vertices 4 5 6 7
    texture roof
  endface
end

# Material flags, in a row west of the blue base. Each is a pair -- the
# control on the east, the flagged copy one panel further west -- so the
# difference is a glance sideways rather than a memory of another map. Every
# panel is the same quad `define`d once below and placed by a `group` whose
# `matref` swaps the whole material, which is also what makes a group's
# material override visible here (`docs/bzw.md`, "Groups"). `passable`
# throughout: these are signboards beside a base, not cover.
#
# The row opens with the two-sided test instead of a flag pair, because
# `noculling` is not how a map asks for a two-sided surface -- see its three
# panels below.

# --- Two sides: one face, one face claiming `noculling`, and two faces.
#
# A one-sided face is gone from behind, and `noculling` does not bring it
# back: a plain `mesh` face draws through `MeshPolySceneNode`, whose `cull`
# (and its base `WallSceneNode::cull`) throws the whole node away when the
# eye is behind its plane, before the flag's `disableCulling()` could ever
# run. The middle panel states the flag and is missing from the south
# anyway, on bzo and on bzflag alike. The third panel is the real answer:
# the same quad written twice with reversed winding, which is what the
# billboard bush and the two animated signs further north already do.
material
  name panel_side
  texture boxwall
  diffuse 1.0 0.85 0.3 1
end

material
  name panel_side_noculling
  texture boxwall
  diffuse 1.0 0.5 0.3 1
  noculling
end

material
  name panel_side_backed
  texture boxwall
  diffuse 0.5 1.0 0.4 1
end

# A tinted texture, and the same tint told not to reach the picture.
# `notexcolor` leaves the texture as it is -- plain boxwall grey -- rather
# than multiplying the mapper's blue through it.
material
  name panel_tinted
  texture boxwall
  diffuse 0.2 0.4 1.0 1
end

material
  name panel_tint_ignored
  texture boxwall
  diffuse 0.2 0.4 1.0 1
  notexcolor
end

# The bush's own shrub cutout on a flat panel. The control blends, cut at
# the threshold it states; the flagged copy draws the same picture opaque --
# black wherever the leaves are not, which is what the channel was hiding.
#
# The flagged copy states no `alphathresh` on purpose. `notexalpha` keeps the
# texture's alpha out of the *blend* and nothing else: a stated threshold is
# an alpha *test*, applied either way (`WallSceneNode.cxx:369-370`), and
# would cut exactly the same pixels away again -- leaving the pair looking
# alike and demonstrating nothing.
material
  name panel_cutout
  texture http://images.bzflag.org/bz_legacy/trepan/tiles/shrub.png
  alphathresh 0.7
end

material
  name panel_cutout_opaque
  texture http://images.bzflag.org/bz_legacy/trepan/tiles/shrub.png
  notexalpha
end

# Half-transparent, for the crossed pair. `nosorting` keeps a translucent
# face writing depth instead of joining the back-to-front pass, so where the
# two quads of one cross pass through each other the nearer one hides the
# further instead of blending with it -- a hard seam up the middle on the
# west cross, an even blend on the east one.
material
  name panel_glass
  texture boxwall
  diffuse 0.6 0.9 1.0 0.45
end

material
  name panel_glass_unsorted
  texture boxwall
  diffuse 0.6 0.9 1.0 0.45
  nosorting
end

# White, so a `tint` on the `group` placing it is the whole of the colour.
material
  name panel_white
  texture boxwall
  diffuse 1 1 1 1
end

# One quad, wound to face north -- towards the map centre, so the row reads
# as a row of signs on the way in from the blue base. One face only, so it
# is there from the north and gone from the south.
define flag_panel_one_face
  mesh
    name Panel
    vertex -2 0 0
    vertex 2 0 0
    vertex 2 0 7
    vertex -2 0 7
    texcoord 0 0
    texcoord 1 0
    texcoord 1 1
    texcoord 0 1
    passable
    face
      vertices 0 3 2 1
      texcoords 0 3 2 1
    endface
  end
enddef

# The same quad with a back face of its own -- the same corners in the
# opposite order, so it carries its own outward plane and survives the cull
# from the south on its own terms. This is what every panel below the
# two-sided test uses, so the whole row reads from either side.
define flag_panel
  mesh
    name Panel
    vertex -2 0 0
    vertex 2 0 0
    vertex 2 0 7
    vertex -2 0 7
    texcoord 0 0
    texcoord 1 0
    texcoord 1 1
    texcoord 0 1
    passable
    face
      vertices 0 3 2 1
      texcoords 0 3 2 1
    endface
    face #backside
      vertices 0 1 2 3
      texcoords 0 1 2 3
    endface
  end
enddef

# Two of those panels crossed at right angles inside one mesh, which is what
# `nosorting` needs to show anything: two separate objects are drawn back to
# front and blend correctly either way, while faces of one mesh are drawn in
# the order they were written and the depth mask decides which wins. Four
# faces, so each quad is two-sided the same way.
define flag_cross
  mesh
    name Cross
    vertex -2 0 0
    vertex 2 0 0
    vertex 2 0 7
    vertex -2 0 7
    vertex 0 -2 0
    vertex 0 2 0
    vertex 0 2 7
    vertex 0 -2 7
    texcoord 0 0
    texcoord 1 0
    texcoord 1 1
    texcoord 0 1
    passable
    face
      vertices 0 3 2 1
      texcoords 0 3 2 1
    endface
    face #backside
      vertices 0 1 2 3
      texcoords 0 1 2 3
    endface
    face
      vertices 4 7 6 5
      texcoords 0 3 2 1
    endface
    face #backside
      vertices 4 5 6 7
      texcoords 0 1 2 3
    endface
  end
enddef

group flag_panel_one_face
  name one_face
  shift -26 -112 0
  matref panel_side
end

group flag_panel_one_face
  name one_face_noculling
  shift -31 -112 0
  matref panel_side_noculling
end

group flag_panel
  name two_faces
  shift -36 -112 0
  matref panel_side_backed
end

group flag_panel
  name texcolor
  shift -46 -112 0
  matref panel_tinted
end

group flag_panel
  name notexcolor
  shift -51 -112 0
  matref panel_tint_ignored
end

group flag_panel
  name texalpha
  shift -61 -112 0
  matref panel_cutout
end

group flag_panel
  name notexalpha
  shift -66 -112 0
  matref panel_cutout_opaque
end

group flag_cross
  name sorting
  shift -76 -112 0
  matref panel_glass
end

group flag_cross
  name nosorting
  shift -81 -112 0
  matref panel_glass_unsorted
end

# `tint` on the instance rather than on the material -- the same white panel
# placed twice, coloured by the `group` line alone. This is how one `define`
# gets placed in several colours without a material per copy.
group flag_panel
  name untinted
  shift -91 -112 0
  matref panel_white
end

group flag_panel
  name tinted
  shift -96 -112 0
  matref panel_white
  tint 1.0 0.3 0.2 1
end

# `phydrv` -- only the two keywords bzo actually reads, matched against
# real map usage rather than upstream's own full set (see "Physics drivers"
# in docs/bzw-plan.md): `linear`, a velocity added to a tank resting on the
# surface naming it, and `death`, which kills a tank outright instead.
# `angular`, `radial` and `slide` are not read -- no real map sampled uses
# any of the three -- so there is nothing to demonstrate for them here.
#
# The two pads deliberately demonstrate two different rules, because
# upstream's own driver checks are two different mechanisms, not one:
#
# `linear` only ever applies once a tank is recorded as *resting* on the
# surface naming it -- upstream's own `LocalPlayer::doUpdateMotion` only
# sets `lastObstacle` (bzo's `lastMotionObstacle`) for a surface a tank was
# actually expelled by (`LocalPlayer.cxx:617-624`, `:771-780`), i.e.
# `location == OnBuilding`, which a flush/drive-through pad can never be --
# it's walked straight through as ordinary ground. So `PD_Conveyor` is real,
# if barely (`size ... 0.1`, comfortably under `_maxBumpHeight`), because a
# flush one would never push at all, on bzo or on upstream.
#
# `death` is checked a different way -- `LocalPlayer::getHitBuilding`/
# `collectInsideBuildings` (LocalPlayer.cxx:927-937, 986-992) run it against
# any face the tank's box currently overlaps, checked directly against
# `MeshFace::inBox`'s own inclusive touching test (MeshFace.cxx:454-461),
# before ever asking whether that face is drive-through. So `PD_Death` is
# flush on purpose (`size ... 0`) -- forced `driveThrough` by bzo's own
# flush-pad rule (see "A pad flush with the ground" in `docs/bzw.md`) -- to
# demonstrate that a death driver reaches a surface a tank drives straight
# across, exactly as it does upstream.
physics
  name test_conveyor
  linear 8 0 0
end

physics
  name test_death
  death Tank Melted By The Test Pad
end

# A `linear` whose push is straight up -- a jump pad, which is the one shape of
# driver upstream gives its own sound (`SFX_BOUNCE`,
# `LocalPlayer.cxx:805-810`), played ahead of the landing and burrow sounds in
# the same else-chain. `ahs3_INCOMING.bzw`'s own cones are the real-map
# precedent (`linear 0 0 30`); 20 here because it is a demo pad rather than a
# route across a field -- just above `_jumpVelocity`, so it reads as a launch
# and not a jump, and the tank is back down in a couple of seconds.
physics
  name test_bounce
  linear 0 0 20
end

# The pads' own look, so each reads visually the way it behaves. Upstream has
# no engine link at all between a `linear` driver and a `textureMatrix` --
# checked directly against `$HOME/bzflag`: neither `PhysicsDriver` nor
# `TextureMatrix` (`src/game/`) names the other anywhere in the tree, and no
# real map sampled pairs a `linear` surface with a `texmat` on the same
# obstacle either -- so a mapper tunes both by hand to agree, the same as
# here: the stripe's own `shift` frequency is `test_conveyor`'s `linear`
# speed (8) divided by a box cap's own tiling (`BOX_TEXTURE_SCALES.capScale`,
# `public/render.js` -- 2 units/tile), so one tile's worth of stripe crosses
# the pad every `capScale / speed` seconds, matching the physical push
# exactly rather than an arbitrary scroll rate.
textureMatrix
  name conveyor_scroll
  shift 4 0
end

material
  name conveyor_look
  addtexture caution
  texmat conveyor_scroll
end

dynamicColor
  name death_pulse
  alpha limits 0.3 1
  alpha sinusoid 1 0 1
end

material
  name death_look
  dyncol death_pulse
  addtexture caution
  nolighting
end

# `automatic_icon`, upstream's own autopilot star -- a marker rather than a
# picture of a surface, which is what a pad a tank is meant to aim for wants.
# It is one sign, so it gets one copy of itself: a box cap tiles every 2 units
# (`BOX_TEXTURE_SCALES.capScale`, `public/render.js`) and this pad is 6 across,
# so `fixedscale 3 3` -- which divides the UVs, `TextureMatrix::finalize`
# (TextureMatrix.cxx:297) -- stretches the three tiles back out into one.
# `fixedcenter` is the pivot that scaling happens about, and upstream defaults
# it to the middle of a single tile (0.5, 0.5); this cap's UVs run 0..3, so
# pivoting there leaves the one copy straddling a corner. Pivoting at the UV
# origin instead sends 0..3 to 0..1, which is the face.
textureMatrix
  name bounce_fit
  fixedscale 3 3
  fixedcenter 0 0
end

material
  name bounce_look
  addtexture automatic_icon
  texmat bounce_fit
  nolighting
end

box
  name PD_Conveyor
  position -135 -100 0
  size 3 3 0.1
  phydrv test_conveyor
  matref conveyor_look
end

# The same barely-real height the other two pads carry, and for a second
# reason besides: a box with a `matref` or `phydrv` is built as a mesh rather
# than a `BoxBuilding` (`isOldBox`, `CustomBox.cxx:304`), and a flush one gives
# that mesh four zero-area side faces, which `MeshFace` discards with an
# "invalid mesh face" warning -- leaving the pad as two coincident horizontal
# faces with nothing to resolve a driver against.
box
  name PD_Death
  position -135 -125 0
  size 3 3 0.1
  phydrv test_death
  matref death_look
end

# South of the purple base against the west wall rather than down in the pad
# column with the other two: a launch wants open sky above it and somewhere
# clear to come down, and the corner below is watchtowers. Same barely-real
# height `PD_Conveyor` needs for the same reason -- a flush pad is walked across
# as ordinary ground and never becomes the `lastMotionObstacle` a `linear` push
# is read off.
box
  name PD_Bounce
  position -135 -35 0
  size 3 3 0.1
  phydrv test_bounce
  matref bounce_look
end

# `dyncol`/`texmat` -- a material's own tint or UV animated from a named
# `dynamicColor`/`textureMatrix` block, resolved once at parse time and
# evaluated fresh every frame by `evaluateDynamicColor`/`applyTextureMatrix`
# in `public/render.js`. See "Animated materials" in `docs/bzw.md` for the
# real maps (`import-Planet-MoFo.com_4202.bzw`'s own `stripetrix`/
# `billboard*`) this is validated against. Two signs, one per mechanism, at
# the field spot the physics driver pads freed up when they moved to the
# west wall above: a scrolling stripe (`texmat`) and a colour-cycling beacon
# (`dyncol`), built the same double-sided, `passable` quad `billboard_bush`
# already demonstrates.
textureMatrix
  name stripe_scroll
  shift 0 0.3
end

material
  name stripe_sign
  addtexture caution
  texmat stripe_scroll
  nolighting
end

dynamicColor
  name beacon_cycle
  red limits 0.2 1
  red sinusoid 2 0 1
  green limits 0.2 1
  green sinusoid 2 0.67 1
  blue limits 0.2 1
  blue sinusoid 2 1.33 1
end

material
  name beacon_sign
  dyncol beacon_cycle
  addtexture caution
  nolighting
end

define anim_stripe_quad
  mesh
    vertex -1.5 0 0
    vertex 1.5 0 0
    vertex 1.5 0 7
    vertex -1.5 0 7
    texcoord 0 0
    texcoord 1 0
    texcoord 1 1
    texcoord 0 1
    passable
    face
      vertices 0 1 2 3
      texcoords 0 1 2 3
      matref stripe_sign
    endface
    face #backside
      vertices 1 0 3 2
      texcoords 1 0 3 2
      matref stripe_sign
    endface
  end
enddef

group anim_stripe_quad
  shift -45 -70 0
end

define anim_beacon_quad
  mesh
    vertex -1.5 0 0
    vertex 1.5 0 0
    vertex 1.5 0 7
    vertex -1.5 0 7
    texcoord 0 0
    texcoord 1 0
    texcoord 1 1
    texcoord 0 1
    passable
    face
      vertices 0 1 2 3
      texcoords 0 1 2 3
      matref beacon_sign
    endface
    face #backside
      vertices 1 0 3 2
      texcoords 1 0 3 2
      matref beacon_sign
    endface
  end
enddef

group anim_beacon_quad
  shift -35 -70 0
end

# All six primitives that expand to a mesh upstream are read now --
# `tetra`, `cone`/`meshpyr`, `arc`/`meshbox`, and `sphere` -- see
# `docs/bzw-plan.md`'s "Mesh geometry" for what is still left on the mesh
# side generally (mainly a perf pass merging same-material triangles).
# Clear of every other test, a free strip north of the centre rings and
# short of the north base.
arc
  name test_arc
  position -75 70 0
  size 8 8 6
  rotation 30
  divisions 16
  angle 270
end

# `arc`'s own `ratio` < 1 -- a genuinely hollow tube (`ArcObstacle::makeRing`)
# rather than the solid wedge/disc `ratio`'s default of 1 collapses to
# (`makePie`, what the plain `test_arc` above exercises). A wedge too, so
# this is also the only fixture testing `startside`/`endside` on an `arc`.
arc
  name test_arc_ring
  position 105 85 0
  size 8 8 6
  ratio 0.5
  divisions 16
  angle 300
end

cone
  name test_cone
  position -45 70 0
  size 6 6 10
  divisions 12
end

sphere
  name test_sphere
  position -15 70 6
  radius 6
  divisions 4
end

# `hemisphere` -- the flat cap that closes it sits at `position`'s own
# height rather than `radius` below the dome's own top, unlike a full
# sphere (`test_sphere` above sets `position`'s own z to its radius so the
# whole thing clears the ground; this one leaves it at zero so the cap does
# instead).
sphere
  name test_sphere_hemi
  position -45 90 0
  radius 6
  divisions 4
  hemisphere
end

tetra
  name test_tetra
  vertex 10 65 0
  vertex 20 65 0
  vertex 15 75 0
  vertex 15 70 8
end

meshbox
  name test_meshbox
  position 45 70 0
  size 6 6 6
  rotation 15
end

meshpyr
  name test_meshpyr
  position 75 70 0
  size 6 6 8
end

# One of `hix.bzw`'s own support trusses, copied whole, with a round platform
# on top of it. Placed in the open ground south of the green base -- clear of
# the flag rings, of the base itself, and of the step/stack tests further
# south -- so bzo carries a worked example of the shape a real map builds out
# of nothing but plain pyramids: a vertical core spike, two crossed ribs, and
# two cap pieces flipped into an inverted bracket flaring up to meet whatever
# the truss holds. On hix that is a walkway deck at z=14; here it is the
# platform below, at the same height.
#
# The textures are hix's too, pulled from the same host that map already
# names. `strut`'s metal is the truss's own, and the platform wears what a hix
# walkway deck wears: concrete on top, the see-through grid underneath so the
# truss reads through it from below, and the stock `caution` stripe around the
# rim, which is what a walkway puts on the edge you can fall off.
material
  name strut
  addtexture http://images.bzflag.org/bz_legacy/louman/thegauntlet/Metal5.png
end

material
  name concrete
  addtexture http://images.bzflag.org/pmatous/concrete-jigsaw-light.png
end

material
  name seethrough
  addtexture http://images.bzflag.org/gbratley/Spazzy%20McGee_fence-barbs.png
end

define support
  pyramid
    name core
    position 0.0 0.0 0.0
    rotation 45.0
    size     2.0 2.0 18.0
    sides matref strut
  end
  pyramid
    name rib0
    position 0.0 0.0 0.0
    rotation 0.0
    size     8.0 1.0 5.0
    sides matref strut
  end
  pyramid
    name rib90
    position 0.0 0.0 0.0
    rotation 90.0
    size     8.0 1.0 5.0
    sides matref strut
  end
  # Flipped with a non-negative height, so `position.z` is the apex and the
  # base lands at `position.z + height` -- 12.0 plus 2.0 puts it flush at
  # 14.0, under the platform. The negative depth is deliberate: the flip
  # mirrors the mesh and a second negative axis cancels that mirror back to a
  # proper rotation, keeping every face correctly wound and lit.
  pyramid
    name cap0
    position 0.0 0.0 12.0
    rotation 0.0
    size     8.0 -0.5 2.0
    flipz
    sides matref strut
  end
  pyramid
    name cap90
    position 0.0 0.0 12.0
    rotation 90.0
    size     8.0 -0.5 2.0
    flipz
    sides matref strut
  end
enddef

group support
  name tower
  shift 100 -42 0
end

# The hazard band around the platform below, marching around the rim. The
# `anim_stripe_quad` sign on the field is the same mechanism on a flat quad;
# this is it on a curved surface, where the shift runs along the rim rather
# than up a face, so one texture matrix is tested against both. Slow on
# purpose -- it marks an edge to watch out for rather than asking to be
# looked at.
textureMatrix
  name platform_stripe_scroll
  shift 0.2 0
end

material
  name platform_caution
  addtexture caution
  texmat platform_stripe_scroll
end

# The same band read from the inside, which needs the opposite shift to travel
# the same way around the platform. A ring's inside wall runs its texcoords
# *down* around the ring where the outside wall runs them up -- upstream's own
# index math, `((divisions + (x % 2)) * 2) - (x + (i * 2))` against the outside
# face's `x + (i * 2)` (`ArcObstacle::makeRing`) -- so one `shift` drives the
# two faces in opposite directions and they have to be written as a pair.
textureMatrix
  name platform_stripe_scroll_inner
  shift -0.2 0
end

material
  name platform_caution_inner
  addtexture caution
  texmat platform_stripe_scroll_inner
end

# A metre-thick disc resting on the bracket, sixteen divisions rather than
# hix's own eight so it reads as round rather than octagonal. Radius 8 puts
# its rim exactly over the cap pieces' own reach, so the bracket meets it at
# the edge instead of stopping short under the middle of it.
arc
  name tower_platform
  position 100 -42 14
  size     8.0 8.0 1.0
  divisions 16
  top matref concrete
  bottom matref seethrough
  outside matref platform_caution
end

# The same band facing the other way, so the rim reads from under the deck as
# well as from outside it. A mesh face is single-sided and there is no flag
# that changes it: `noculling` sets GL state that a plain face never reaches,
# because `MeshPolySceneNode::cull` (`MeshPolySceneNode.cxx:255`) drops the
# whole node first -- "cull if eye is behind (or on) plane" -- and only a mesh
# drawn from a `drawInfo` block escapes that test. So the second side has to be
# geometry, and a ring is the stock way to write it: a solid `arc` has no
# inside edge at all (`ArcObstacle.cxx:97`, and `materials[Inside]` appears
# only inside `makeRing`), while a ring's inside wall carries the inward normal
# `n = (-cos, -sin)` (`:454`) this needs.
#
# Which side you see is decided per face by where your eye is, not by where the
# platform is: from outside, the disc's own rim faces you and this ring's does
# not; from anywhere inside the rim's cylinder -- looking up through the
# `seethrough` underside -- it is the other way round, and the far side of the
# band shows through the fence. One face of the pair, always, from every angle.
#
# Wholly inside the disc (radius 7.85 to 7.95 against its 8, and 14.02 to 14.98
# against its 14 to 15) so no face of it is coplanar with the disc's rim, top or
# bottom: coincident faces would trade the missing band for depth fighting.
# `passable` because it is a decoration -- the disc around it is what a tank
# drives on and what a shot hits. `ratio` is upstream's own wall fraction,
# `inrad = size * (1 - ratio)` (`ArcObstacle.cxx:153`), not an inner radius.
arc
  name tower_platform_band
  position 100 -42 14.02
  size     7.95 7.95 0.96
  ratio    0.0125
  divisions 16
  passable
  matref platform_caution_inner
end

# A spinning tank over the map centre.
#
# Where the model comes from, since it is the obvious question: it is
# BZFlag's own stock tank -- upstream's `misc/tank.obj`, which this repo
# already ships as `public/obj/tank.obj` and draws every player with. The
# `define` below is generated from that file by `scripts/gen-tank-bzw.mjs`
# and nothing else: 174 vertices, 106 normals, 153 texcoords, 326 corners and
# 274 triangles. Re-run that script to rebuild it.
#
# The *idea* is Rat's Nest's (`RatsNest.bzw`, KyleVE/Winny, 2007), which
# hangs six spinning copies of that same BZFlag tank around its map and is
# where the name `SpinTank` comes from. Its geometry is not copied here and
# must not be: that map is CC BY-NC-SA 2.5, whose non-commercial term does
# not sit with this project's AGPL -- the same reason `dw_missilewar3.bzw` is
# gitignored rather than bundled. Both files turn out to hold the same
# upstream model anyway, to the same 8.04 by 2.80 footprint and the same 274
# triangles, so generating from our own copy costs nothing and asks nobody's
# permission.
#
# `drawInfo` is where upstream keeps a mesh's render geometry and the only
# place it reads `angvel` from, and a mesh may state nothing else -- every
# tank in `RatsNest.bzw` is written this way, with no `face` of its own at
# all. bzo draws it from the same block (see `readMeshDrawInfoLine` in
# server.js) and, like upstream, collides against the face list instead,
# which here is empty: the model is decoration a tank drives through. Written
# this way rather than as plain faces on purpose, so the same text spins the
# same tank in a real bzflag client and here.
#
# No `size` on the instance, because none is needed: the model already
# measures 2.800 across and 2.080 tall, which are `_tankWidth` and
# `_tankHeight` (`TANK_WIDTH`/`TANK_HEIGHT`, server/collision.cjs) to within
# a hair. Its 8.04 of length is the 6.0 body plus the barrel reaching past
# it.
#
# `angvel 45` turns it once every eight seconds about wherever the instance
# placed its local origin -- here the map centre, over the flag pads
# (docs/bzw.md, "Mesh transforms" and #88).
#
# Height: a jump reaches `jumpVelocity^2 / (2 * gravity)` = 19^2 / 19.6 =
# 18.42, and the instance sits at three quarters of that. The model stands on
# its own origin, the way `tank.obj` is drawn, so the shift *is* the height
# of its underside -- no offset to work back out of the vertex list. It
# states no face at all, so a tank that does jump into it passes through.
# The camo every bzo tank wears, untinted. `rogue_tank` is the base picture
# the renderer colours per team at runtime (`loadTintedTexture`/
# `getBaseTeamTint`, public/texture.js); left alone it is the grey-and-black
# pattern a rogue drives, which is what this one should look like -- it
# belongs to no team. White diffuse rather than Rat's Nest's own flat 0.2
# grey, so the texture arrives at full brightness instead of being multiplied
# down to nearly black. The model's own 641 texcoords do the placing, in the
# per-island layout it was unwrapped with, so the pattern does not run
# continuously across every seam. Treads do not roll: that animation belongs
# to a real tank's own render path, not to a mesh obstacle.
material
  name Tank
  texture rogue_tank
  diffuse 1 1 1 1
end

define SpinTank
